Hi everyone.  This is my first unblemished ascension.  I ascended a
Valkyrie prior to this, however that ascension wasn't entirely
unblemished.  I had emailed her save game home so I could play over
the weekend.  After playing at home for a few hours, Nethack crashed,
and after the recovery she met her fate by trickery.  Unable to let
her go, I restored her from the emailed copy and continued on to an
ascension.

This one was played entirely at home, though.  I love all the story
line posts for this sort of thing, but I think I'll present this as a
list of mistakes made and still persevered enough to ascend.

Jelaad the Gnome Wizard started out innocuously enough with all the
usual Wizard starting gear.  Having killed about twenty Wizards prior
to this he was descending slowly and carefully.  He skipped Gnomish
mines and drove straight down the main dungeon.  He stopped into
Sokoban briefly and acquired an amulet of reflection.  He would wear
this for quite a while until he got his SDSM and amulet of life
saving.

Once he completed Sokoban he went back up to start down the Mines.  An
altar somewhere in here provided Magicbane which would become his main
weapon throughout the game.  Frequent engraving of Elbereth saved his
life many times.  The Mines were completed without incident and he
ascended back to the main dungeon with his brand new blessed
luckstone.

So far, he had not found any wands of wishing or magic lamps.  In
fact, this trend would continue throughout the game.  He finally found
a single magic lamp which he blessed and used as a light source
throughout Gehennon.  The single wand he found was the Castle wand.

So we rejoin our intrepid adventurer somewhere below the big room and
the oracle.  His gear was quite good for generated stuff.  He had
picked up speed boots along the line somewhere.  Along with his magic
cloak, some enchanted studded leather armor, and a nice enchanted
elven leather helm, he had no real armor worries.

There were quite a few altars throughout the lower part of the
dungeon, sometimes even two or more on a single level.  At one altar
around Dlvl 12, we will start off our mistake list.  Our hero was
happily using up the large number of monster creation items he had
found during his descent.  At one point, he applied a bag of tricks
and found himself surrounded by monsters.  Every time one died, he
would grab the corpse, step on the altar, and #offer it to Thoth. 
This habit was quickly increasing his luck to max.  He was also
praying frequently to fill out his spellbook collection.  Well,
unnoticed by him, and indeed I didn't notice either since I hadn't
been playing Gnome characters very much, a Gnome lord had spawned and
fallen to a force bolt.  Jelaad grabbed the corpse and sacrificed it
thus thoroughly angering his God, converting the altar to Chaotic, and
attaching a ball and chain to his ankle.  Despite this major setback,
and the additional hour or so of sacrificing, our hero eventually
restored himself to favor in the eyes of his god, and continued on
down the dungeon.

The adventure was uneventful until the castle level.  Our hero had
made himself a suit of SDSM thanks to a Dragon in Ludios.  The quest
was simple, and the additional power regeneration helped immensely. 
In the castle, thanks to some persistent poly-piling, our hero got
himself a brand new Hawaiian shirt, gloves of dexterity, and a helm of
Brilliance.  A full ascension kit was now held without using a single
wish.  The castle wand was used for a magic marker, and the armor was
enchanted up to appropriate levels.

Being full of himself, and after finally getting a hold of a ring of
poly-control, our hero decides to try eating some rings and amulets. 
He gets back up to his stash and poly-piles his large collection of
useless rings and amulets until he finds a few.  Thus starts the next
mistake.

Having never polymorphed himself before, or actually any character at
all, he neglected to take off his armor before polymorphing himself
into a rust monster.  This, of course, destroyed his SDSM, Hawaiian
shirt, Cloak of Magic Resistance, and caused all the rest to drop to
the floor, including Magicbane.  Cursing his own stupidity, and
wondering what he would do, our hero consumed the only amulet that he
could and didn't get the innate magic breathing.

Now we start a long comical series of mistakes.  Not knowing any other
way to un-polymorph himself, he polymorphs to a Gnome.  He feels like
a new man, and notices his level has decreased to about 18, and his
stats are rearranged.  Well, that's interesting, let's try
polymorphing to a Gnome a few more times and see if that restores his
levels.  Well, our hero was reduced to about level 15 before he gave
up.  Several of his most powerful enchantments had been made
unavailable due to lack of power and lower skill levels, and he was
still naked.  Well, there just happened to be a co-aligned temple on
this level and he decides to go get his AC below 0 now that it would
cost a lot less.

By the time he got into the next room, naked, he discovered a Vampire
Lord and one or two other monsters.  You can imagine my incredulous
look as every single time the Vampire Lord hit him, our hero lost
another level.  By the time he hit level 9 I scribbled Elbereth, and
managed to get back to my armor and weapon.  I dispatched the monsters
easily enough, and finished buying protection.  Needless to say, the
protection was MUCH cheaper at level 9.

Let's skip ahead a ways.  Our hero had managed to get back to the
lofty level of 15, with about half the power and hitpoints he once
enjoyed.  He found a set of Blue Dragon scales in Gehennon somewhere
and got himself a set of BDSM.  Also, another t-shirt was acquired
through poly-piling.  A new cloak was acquired thanks to a former
hapless Wizard who met his fate in the Gnomish mines.  (I barely
remembered finding these bones and level-ported back to get the cursed
cloak).  Through some helpfully placed nurses, his hitpoints made
their way back to 240 and his AC was a decent -43.

Vlad's tower was completed without incident, the vibrating square was
found and mapped, and I moved back up to the Wizard's tower in
preparation for my ascension run.

In the Wizard's tower, I finally noticed some death bolts whizzing
around.  Panicking at this, I started the next series of unfortunate
incidents.  I still had two wishes; one in the wand, and one in my
trusty magic lamp.  I convinced myself that I needed SDSM again for
reflection.  I wished for a +2 set of SDSM, and used the last wish for
a magic marker.  I used two scrolls and got the SDSM to +4.  Now, I
thought that there was a pretty good chance to get it higher (it was
late at night), so I read another scroll and vaporized my brand new
set of armor.  Also, I had used up about half of my marker on the
scrolls to enchant it, so now I had just about wasted two whole wishes
and I didn't have much hope of getting anymore wishes since I had
pretty much killed everything in the dungeon.

Sighing in frustration, I finished enchanting my BDSM, and put that
back on, pretty much putting myself back to where I was before I used
those wishes.

I saved at that point and waited for the following day before
continuing.  I didn't want this story to end as a YASD somewhere deep
in the already cleared dungeon.

Skip ahead, and I'm ready for the ascension run.  At the entrance to
the Wizard's tower, I find a nurse.  There aren't any monsters around,
so I engrave Elbereth, take off my armor, and start enjoying her
ministrations.  Well, a demon of some type had joined the party but
was held at bay by Elbereth, so I ignored him.  You can imagine my
horror when he summoned Demogorgon who started sickening me right
away.  Thus began my odyssey to slay the foul Demogorgon.  Liberal
applications of my Unicorn Horn kept me healthy.  I found that
engraving Elbereth, then curing myself, worked nicely.  I would take a
few steps toward the entrance stairs, Demo would port in and sicken
me, I would engrave and recover, then hit him once to get him to
teleport away from me.  Lather, rinse, and repeat all the way across
the level until I got to stand on the stairs themselves, engrave
Elbereth, and kill him once and for all.

I go through the Wizard's tower, kill Rodney, get the book, and get
back down to the VS.  Everything goes according to plan and I make my
way through the sanctum.  The fight with the final priestess was very
tough, our hero came close to buying it there, especially with his
reduced level and hit points.  But he prevailed.  Murphy's law firmly
in place, I discover that his ring of conflict and ring of slow
digestion had disappeared sometime during the fight.  I presume they
got destroyed thanks to the lack of reflection.

The run to the top was uneventful.  Finally, entering the plains, I
find that he is pretty thoroughly outmatched.  The amulet was draining
his few remaining power points, which were at about 180 at this point,
so casting spells wasn't useful for very long.  I decided to cut and
run to the portal as quickly as possible.  A few confused readings of
gold detection on each level provides the quick escape routes, and
Jelaad gets to the Astral plain without clearing the previous plains. 
He comes close to buying it again on the plain of air when he spots a
silver dragon and tries to kill it to replace his long lost armor.  
That plan was abandoned, and he quickly exited the plain.

Astral was extremely tough.  Without the power points to support spell
casting, without his ring of conflict, he was forced to charm a bunch
of monsters, teleport the dangerous ones out of the way and try to
kill Famine by hand.  Fortunately for our hero, I choose the correct
branch of the level and we go straight to the altar.  Famine actually
killed him once, but the "oLS saved him, and he had a replacement in
his pack.  Famine died twice, and then I teleported the remaining
monsters out of the way and quickly offered the amulet up to Thoth.

I hope this wasn't too long for you all.  Thanks for reading if you're
still here.  I didn't get a dump of the final stats, but they would
have been typical and something you've read before anyway.

Our hero met his reward as a level 15 Wizard with 5 million points. 
He had lots of gems in his pack.

